        "insufficient_evidence": true,
        "summary": "Robin’s example was a leader acknowledging three consecutive months of lost market share, admitting he did not know exactly what was happening or what to do, and saying he had never needed the team more. The supplied evidence does not mention a missed product deadline.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Example in the evidence",
            "content": "Robin described a leader telling the team that they had lost market share for the third month in a row, that he wished he could say he knew exactly what was happening and what they should do, but he did not—and that he had never needed them more. She contrasted this with pretending nothing was wrong. She also cautioned that appropriate vulnerability is not the same as undermining confidence by saying, for example, that the leader was unsure he should be VP of marketing.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": [
          "Carole Robin’s example of a leader expressing vulnerability after a missed product deadline"
        ]
